      "text": "To find \\(\\sin \\frac{11\\pi}{3}\\), we start by simplifying the angle \\(\\frac{11\\pi}{3}\\) to an equivalent angle within the interval \\([0, 2\\pi)\\).\n\nFirst, we express \\(\\frac{11\\pi}{3}\\) in terms of a multiple of \\(2\\pi\\):\n\n\\[\n\\frac{11\\pi}{3} = 2\\pi + \\frac{\\pi}{3}\n\\]\n\nSince \\(2\\pi\\) represents one full rotation around the unit circle, adding \\(2\\pi\\) does not change the position of the angle. Therefore, \\(\\sin \\frac{11\\pi}{3} = \\sin \\left(2\\pi + \\frac{\\pi}{3}\\right)\\).\n\nUsing the periodicity of the sine function, which is \\(2\\pi\\), we know:\n\\[\n\\sin \\left(2\\pi + \\frac{\\pi}{3}\\right) = \\sin \\frac{\\pi}{3}\n\\]\n\nNext, we need to determine \\(\\sin \\frac{\\pi}{3}\\).\n\nRecall the values of sine for common angles:\n\\[\n\\sin \\frac{\\pi}{6} = \\frac{1}{2}\n\\]\n\\[\n\\sin \\frac{\\pi}{3} = \\sin \\left(\\frac{\\pi}{2} - \\frac{\\pi}{6}\\right)\n\\]\n\nUsing the sine subtraction formula:\n\\[\n\\sin \\left(\\frac{\\pi}{2} - x\\right) = \\cos x\n\\]\n\nWe get:\n\\[\n\\sin \\frac{\\pi}{3} = \\cos \\frac{\\pi}{6}\n\\]\n\nFinally, the value of \\(\\cos \\frac{\\pi}{6}\\) is:\n\\[\n\\cos \\frac{\\pi}{6} = \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}\n\\]\n\nThus, we have:\n\\[\n\\sin \\frac{\\pi}{3} = \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}\n\\]\n\nTherefore,\n\\[\n\\sin \\frac{11\\pi}{3} = \\sin \\left(2\\pi + \\frac{\\pi}{3}\\right) = \\sin \\frac{\\pi}{3} = \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}\n\\]\n\nThe final answer is:\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}}\n\\]",
