      "text": "   \n[List the steps to solve the problem and the reasoning behind each step.]\n\n[Example: \\(45^\\circ\\) and \\(135^\\circ\\) are complementary angles. \\(\\sin \\frac{11\\pi}{3}\\) is equivalent to \\(\\sin 135^\\circ\\) due to the periodic and symmetric property of the unit circle.] Let's solve the problem step-by-step.\n\n### Step 1: Reduce the angle within the principal range.\nThe angle \\(\\frac{11\\pi}{3}\\) is given and needs to be reduced within the principal range of the sine function, which is \\(0 \\leq \\theta < 2\\pi\\).\n\n\\[\n\\frac{11\\pi}{3} \\mod 2\\pi = \\frac{11\\pi}{3} - 2\\pi \\cdot \\left\\lfloor \\frac{11\\pi}{3}/2\\pi \\right\\rfloor\n\\]\n\n\\[\n= \\frac{11\\pi}{3} - 2\\pi \\cdot (3)\n\\]\n\n\\[\n= \\frac{11\\pi}{3} - 6\\pi\n\\]\n\n\\[\n= \\frac{11\\pi}{3} - \\frac{18\\pi}{3}\n\\]\n\n\\[\n= \\frac{11\\pi - 18\\pi}{3}\n\\]\n\n\\[\n= \\frac{-7\\pi}{3}\n\\]\n\nSince \\(\\frac{-7\\pi}{3}\\) is within the range \\(0 \\leq \\theta < 2\\pi\\), we can use this angle directly for the sine function.\n\n### Step 2: Use the periodicity and symmetry of the sine function.\nWe know that \\(\\sin(\\theta) = \\sin(\\theta + 2k\\pi)\\) for any integer \\(k\\).\n\n\\[\n\\sin\\left(\\frac{11\\pi}{3}\\right) = \\sin\\left(\\frac{-7\\pi}{3}\\right) = \\sin\\left(\\frac{2\\pi - \\frac{7\\pi}{3}}{3}\\right)\n\\]\n\n\\[\n= \\sin\\left(\\frac{6\\pi - 7\\pi}{9}\\right)\n\\]\n\n\\[\n= \\sin\\left(\\frac{-\\pi}{9}\\right)\n\\]\n\nSince \\(\\sin(-x) = -\\sin(x)\\),\n\n\\[\n\\sin\\left(\\frac{-7\\pi}{3}\\right) = \\sin\\left(\\frac{7\\pi}{3}\\right) = -\\sin\\left(\\frac{\\pi}{3}\\right)\n\\]\n\n### Step 3: Calculate \\(\\sin\\left(\\frac{\\pi}{3}\\right)\\).\n\nWe know that \\(\\sin\\left(\\frac{\\pi}{3}\\right) = \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}\\).\n\nTherefore,\n\n\\[\n\\sin\\left(\\frac{11\\pi}{3}\\right) = -\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}\n\\]\n\n### Final Answer:\n\\[\n\\boxed{-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}}\n\\]",
